te that institutional quality is not proportional to README length: the front page is the **control surface for the documentation system**, not a replacement for the documentation system. citeturn15search0turn15search1turn15search2turn15search3\n\nFor Continuum, the central narrative should also be corrected against the current agent-infrastructure landscape. It is no longer defensible to claim that existing agent systems lack persistence, memory, long-running execution, guardrails, tracing, or durable workflows in general. OpenAI's Agents SDK now provides sessions, guardrails, tracing, resumable/sandbox execution paths, and integrations for durable agents; Google's ADK explicitly models sessions, state, long-term memory, events, artifacts, and ambient asynchronous agents; Temporal provides durable replay, retry policy, long-running workflows, and failure recovery. citeturn13search1turn13search4turn13search7turn13search25turn13search2turn13search8turn13search14turn13search17turn13search3turn13search21\n\nThat changes Continuum's strongest defensible position. The opportunity is not “agents finally persist.” The potentially differentiating proposition is a **principal-centric operating architecture that jointly separates cognition, durable identity, environment, authority, consequential execution, evidence, recovery, and institutional coordination**. That synthesis should be presented as a design contribution and then proven component by component. Novelty should not be declared merely because the names are new. Anthropic's work on long-running agent harnesses, tool design, context engineering, and multi-agent architectures, together with the current OpenAI and Google agent stacks, demonstrates how quickly the baseline is moving. citeturn14search1turn14search4turn14search7turn14search9turn14search17turn14search19\n\nThe highest-value Continuum front page should therefore make one architectural statement unmistakable:\n\n> **The principal persists. Components may change. Authority does not emerge from cognition. External effects require independent admission. Uncertainty remains explicit until evidence resolves it.**\n\nThat is more technically credible than “revolutionary agent platform.” It describes a set of falsifiable system properties.\n\nThe README should use a five-layer publication structure:\n\n1. **Identity and activation:** exact technical classification, maturity, minimal badges, navigation, two-paragraph abstract, and a fast path to running or observing the system.\n2. **Problem and differentiation:** current industry baseline, residual architectural gap, DCA primitives, capabilities, non-goals, and what those primitives enable.\n3. **Mechanism:** topology, state ownership, effect path, recovery semantics, security boundary, mathematical invariants, resource/performance model.\n4. **Evidence:** qualification matrix, tests, benchmarks, threat model, attestations, reproducibility, known limitations.\n5. **Institutional surface:** roadmap, contributing, security reporting, citation, license, governance, accessibility, releases, SBOM, and provenance.\n\nGitHub can render Mermaid diagrams natively and supports collapsible `<details>` sections, workflow badges, light/dark-aware image variants, and standard Markdown. It does **not** provide a general arbitrary-JavaScript or iframe execution surface inside README Markdown; GitHub-Flavored Markdown filters disallowed raw HTML elements such as `script`, `style`, and `iframe`. Rich interactive architecture explorers therefore belong in GitHub Pages, Codespaces, or another linked application rather than inside the README itself. citeturn1search0turn1search14turn7search3turn11search0\n\nA high-authority presentation stack for Continuum is therefore:\n\n```text\nREADME.md\n│\n├── Exact system definition\n├── Status + evidence-linked badges\n├── Abstract\n├── Problem → primitive → effect → evidence\n├── DCA topology\n├── 60-second activation path\n├── Safety / performance contracts\n├── Verification matrix\n└── Roadmap + institutional links\n       │\n       ├── docs/\n       │    ├── architecture/\n       │    ├── threat-model/\n       │    ├── protocols/\n       │    ├── benchmarks/\n       │    ├── adr/\n       │    └── operations/\n       │\n       ├── GitHub Actions → tested claims\n       ├── Codespaces → executable environment\n       ├── GitHub Pages → interactive architecture / demo\n       ├── Releases → signed/attested artifacts\n       └── SECURITY / CONTRIBUTING / CITATION / LICENSE\n```\n\nThe most important publication rule is:\n\n> **Never let prose outrun evidence.**\n\nAn “Implemented” capability should resolve to code and tests. A performance number should resolve to benchmark methodology and raw results. A security claim should resolve to a threat model or verification artifact. A “guarantee” should identify the assumptions under which it holds. A planned capability should be labeled Planned. A research hypothesis should be labeled Research.\n\nThat standard will make Continuum look more authoritative than extravagant language could.\n\n## Evidence from leading repositories and the current AI systems baseline\n\n### What leading README front pages actually do\n\nThe patterns below are more useful than attempting to imitate the visual design of one repository.\n\n| Pattern | Strong reference | Engagement | Institutional authority | Maintenance cost | Continuum recommendation |\n|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---|\n| Exact one-sentence technical definition | PyTorch, vLLM | High | High | Low | **Mandatory** |\n| Two-paragraph abstract explaining problem and mechanism | AlphaFold / research repositories | High | Very high | Low | **Mandatory** |\n| Fast executable quickstart | Transformers, vLLM | Very high | High | Medium | **Mandatory once runnable** |\n| Explicit limitations / scope boundary | AlphaFold, Transformers | Medium | Very high | Low | **Mandatory** |\n| Architecture diagram + component model | Systems repositories; GitHub native Mermaid | High | Very high | Medium | **Mandatory** |\n| Curated CI/release/security badge row | GitHub Actions ecosystem | Medium | High when truthful | Low-medium | **Use 4–7 badges** |\n| Paper / citation / technical report path | AlphaFold, vLLM | Medium | Very high | Low | **Strongly recommended** |\n| Interactive executable environment | Codespaces | Very high | High | Medium | **Recommended** |\n| Interactive visual documentation | GitHub Pages | High | High | Medium-high | **Recommended** |\n| Huge feature inventory above the fold | Common in tooling repos | Medium initially | Low if unverified | High | **Avoid** |\n| Multilingual README variants | Transformers | High reach | Medium | High | Add only after canonical English stabilizes |\n| Deep build internals in root README | PyTorch-style large project docs | Medium | High | High | Move detailed internals to `/docs`; keep front page navigable |\n\nThese observations come directly from the current vLLM, PyTorch, Transformers, and AlphaFold repositories and GitHub's own rendering capabilities. citeturn15search0turn15search1turn15search2turn15search3turn1search0\n\nOne useful negative lesson is maintenance. Even highly mature repositories accumulate stale README references: for example, PyTorch had a 2026 issue concerning an old Slack-invitation path, while an AlphaFold pull request in 2025 corrected an obsolete CUDA image reference. A README should therefore be treated as a tested interface rather than static prose. citeturn15search25turn15search19\n\nEmpirical README research points in the same direction. Studies of open-source READMEs find recurring emphasis on “what” and “how,” while purpose, status, contribution material, and structure vary substantially; research has also associated better-organized documentation and contribution information with healthier project presentation, though such relationships are correlational rather than proof of causation. More recent research has identified outdated README instructions as a meaningful onboarding problem. citeturn9search5turn9search3turn9search15\n\n### The real infrastructure gap Continuum should claim\n\nThe README should **not** use a straw-man comparison such as:\n\n> “Existing agents are stateless chat loops.”\n\nThat is no longer true as a general industry statement.\n\nCurrent systems already provide substantial pieces:\n\n| Existing capability | Current primary-source evidence | Implication for Continuum positioning |\n|---|---|---|\n| Session persistence | OpenAI Agents SDK sessions maintain conversation history across runs. citeturn13search28 | Persistence alone is not differentiation. |\n| State + long-term memory | Google ADK distinguishes session state from long-term memory. citeturn13search2turn13search8 | “Agents need memory” is not a novel thesis. |\n| Versioned artifacts | Google ADK has artifact abstractions for named/versioned binary data. citeturn13search17 | Artifact persistence is established baseline functionality. |\n| Guardrails | OpenAI Agents SDK runs input/output validation and tripwires. citeturn13search4 | Guardrails cannot be presented as unique. |\n| Traceability | OpenAI Agents SDK records model generations, tools, handoffs, guardrails, and custom events. citeturn13search7 | Continuum needs stronger evidence semantics than “has traces.” |\n| Durable workflow recovery | Temporal records event history and uses deterministic replay after failures. citeturn13search3turn13search24 | “Survives restarts” is not enough. |\n| Long-running agent execution | Temporal explicitly supports durable AI workflows; OpenAI also documents durable-agent integrations. citeturn13search21turn13search25 | Continuum needs a stronger principal/authority story. |\n| Ambient/background agents | Google ADK supports asynchronous ambient agents. citeturn13search14 | Background execution itself is no differentiator. |\n| Long-running agent harnesses | Anthropic documents techniques for agents that work across extended horizons. citeturn14search17 | Long horizon is an industry direction, not an exclusive Continuum property. |\n| Multi-agent execution | Anthropic has documented a production multi-agent research architecture. citeturn14search9 | “Multiple agents” is not novel. |\n\nThe stronger gap is **composition of responsibilities and authority semantics**.\n\nA conventional agent runtime can reasonably answer:\n\n> What model is executing?  \n> What conversation state exists?  \n> Which tool was called?  \n> What did the workflow return?\n\nContinuum should aspire to answer a stricter set:\n\n> Which durable principal is responsible?  \n> Which version of its authority was valid?  \n> Which exact operation was authorized?  \n> Which placement was permitted to emit it?  \n> Was a dispatch attempted?  \n> Was the external outcome independently observed?  \n> Is the result committed, denied, failed, or still unknown?  \n> Which evidence justifies that state?  \n> Can a stale process repeat the action after recovery?\n\nThat is a credible architectural axis because it distinguishes **agent behavior** from **operational authority**.\n\nThis distinction has become more important as agents gain real-world action surfaces. OpenAI's public description of ChatGPT agent explicitly discusses increased risk when agents act through connected systems, including prompt injection, sensitive information, and real-world actions, and describes confirmation/supervision controls for consequential operations. citeturn14search6 Anthropic similarly emphasizes that tool design and evaluation materially affect agent performance and reliability. citeturn14search4turn14search11\n\n### The defensible Continuum innovation thesis\n\nThe strongest current thesis is therefore:\n\n**Continuum is not merely an agent framework. It is an attempt to define a durable operating identity and effect-control plane around replaceable cognition.**\n\nIts potentially differentiating primitives are:\n\n| Primitive | Purpose | Closest established ideas | What Continuum must prove |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **VERA durable principal** | Decouple accountable principal identity from model/process/session | service identities, actors, principals, agent sessions | continuity semantics under model/process/placement replacement |\n| **Continuum Computer** | Give a logical execution environment durable ownership separate from its current placement | VMs, containers, actors, durable workers | recovery and stale-placement fencing |\n| **Principal epoch** | Revoke stale authority after security/lifecycle changes | fencing tokens, generations, credential epochs | old epochs cannot produce accepted effects |\n| **Effect Controller** | Put final consequential-effect authority outside cognition | capability systems, policy enforcement points, zero-trust service boundaries | no bypass from model/worker/UI paths |\n| **Immutable operation binding** | Prevent mutable intent from changing after admission | transaction IDs, command envelopes, idempotency keys | exact binding to actor/action/parameters/authority versions |\n| **`UNKNOWN` outcome state** | Represent ambiguous external execution without declaring success/failure | distributed transaction uncertainty, reconciliation workflows | no unsafe blind replay after ambiguity |\n| **Evidence record** | Separate agent narrative from system observation | audit logs, provenance, attestations | evidence integrity and semantic relationship to external outcome |\n| **Regency** | Govern mandates/membership/approvals without conflating them with actual effect rights | IAM, workflow approvals, organizational policy | precise grant derivation and revocation |\n| **DCA** | Decompose autonomous runtime into explicit responsibilities | actor/workflow/OS/security architectures | useful composability and measurable improvement over simpler baseline |\n\nThis is a **novelty hypothesis**, not a proven novelty claim. The closest-prior-art review should be formalized before publication of language such as “first,” “unique,” “new paradigm,” or “revolutionary.”\n\n### What Continuum could unlock if these properties are demonstrated\n\nThe enabling consequences are more credible when written as derivations rather than slogans:\n\n```text\nDurable principal\n    + durable owned state\n    + replaceable cognition\n    → model/process replacement without necessarily creating a new actor\n\nScoped revocable authority\n    + independent effect admission\n    → model capability can increase without automatically increasing effect authority\n\nStable operation identity\n    + reserve-before-send\n    + explicit UNKNOWN\n    + reconciliation\n    → safer recovery from ambiguous external calls\n\nPlacement generations\n    + effect fencing\n    → recovered/stale workers can be prevented from continuing to act\n\nEvidence separated from self-report\n    + provider observation\n    + provenance\n    → operational claims can be independently inspected\n\nExplicit organizations\n    + principal separation\n    + bounded grants\n    → multi-agent coordination need not imply authority pooling\n```\n\nThese are architectural implications, not proof that the current Continuum implementation already provides them.\n\n## Recommended Continuum front-page architecture\n\n### Above-the-fold composition\n\nThe first screen should answer five questions before the reader scrolls deeply:\n\n**What is it?**\n\n> **Continuum — a persistent, governed runtime for autonomous principals built on Distributed Cognitive Architecture.**\n\n**What is structurally different?**\n\n> Separate principal identity, cognition, state, execution environment, authority, effects, and evidence.\n\n**Why should the reader care?**\n\n> Run long-lived autonomous work without treating a model session, available tool, or successful API return as the root of identity, permission, or truth.\n\n**Can it be trusted yet?**\n\nShow a machine-derived status, not a superlative.\n\n**Where can I verify it?**\n\nExpose Architecture, Security, Benchmarks, Evidence, Quickstart, and Roadmap links immediately.\n\nA recommended top block:\n\n```markdown\n# Continuum\n### Persistent, governed runtime infrastructure for autonomous principals.\n\n**Asentxia Systems** · **Architecture:** DCA · **Principal:** VERA · **Governance:** The Regency\n\n[![CI](...)](...)\n[![Security](...)](...)\n[![Coverage](...)](...)\n[![Release](...)](...)\n[![License](...)](...)\n[![Open in Codespaces](https://github.com/codespaces/badge.svg)](...)\n\n**[Architecture](...) · [Quickstart](...) · [Security](...) · [Evidence](...) · [Benchmarks](...) · [Roadmap](...)**\n\n> **The principal persists. Components may change.**\n> Authority does not emerge from cognition. Unknown outcomes do not become successful outcomes by assertion.\n```\n\nGitHub provides first-party workflow status badges, while Codespaces supports an “Open in GitHub Codespaces” badge/deep link. GitHub's dev-container support uses `.devcontainer/devcontainer.json` to define repeatable development environments, including tools, extensions, and forwarded ports. citeturn0search0turn10search0turn0search1\n\n### Use progressive disclosure, not maximal front-page density\n\nThe README should remain dense but tier information by decision value:\n\n```text\nLevel A — 15 seconds\nName → classification → value → status → evidence\n\nLevel B — 2 minutes\nProblem → differentiation → architecture → capabilities → quickstart\n\nLevel C — 10 minutes\nSecurity → invariants → performance → recovery → verification\n\nLevel D — deep review\nADRs → protocol specs → threat model → benchmark raw data\nformal tests → release attestations → source\n```\n\nGitHub automatically generates a document outline/table of contents from headings and supports `<details>` blocks, so large secondary material such as protocol examples, environment matrices, benchmark methodology, and provider-specific setup can remain available without overwhelming the primary narrative. citeturn1search1turn1search14\n\n### Visual system\n\nUse four classes of visual, each with a specific job.\n\n**Hero architecture image:** one manually designed SVG or PNG showing the principal/effect/evidence boundary. Supply dark/light variants with `<picture>` if necessary. GitHub documents use of `picture`/`prefers-color-scheme` for theme-aware assets. citeturn7search3turn7search6\n\n**Mermaid topology:** source-controlled and rendered natively by GitHub. citeturn1search0\n\n**Mermaid sequence:** prove the effect-control logic rather than merely presenting boxes.\n\n**External interactive explorer:** build a richer DCA graph, traces, benchmark viewer, or simulated operation inspector on GitHub Pages and link to it using a clear call to action. GitHub Pages supports static HTML/CSS/JavaScript sites and can be deployed through GitHub Actions, while arbitrary interactive script/iframe content is not an appropriate README-native mechanism because GitHub sanitizes those raw HTML constructs. citeturn10search17turn10search5turn11search0\n\nA useful architecture visual:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TB\n    OWNER[\"Human / Organization\"]\n    REG[\"The Regency<br/>mandates · membership · approvals\"]\n    VERA[\"VERA<br/>durable principal\"]\n    COG[\"Cognition<br/>model · planner · context\"]\n    STATE[\"Authoritative State<br/>tasks · grants · operations · evidence\"]\n    COMP[\"Continuum Computer<br/>logical execution environment\"]\n    WORKER[\"Worker / Sandbox<br/>untrusted or restricted execution\"]\n    EC[\"Effect Controller<br/>independent effect admission\"]\n    EXT[\"External Systems<br/>APIs · services · infrastructure\"]\n    OBS[\"Observation / Reconciliation\"]\n    EVID[\"Evidence Store\"]\n\n    OWNER --> REG\n    REG --> STATE\n    STATE --> VERA\n    VERA --> COG\n    VERA --> COMP\n    COMP --> WORKER\n\n    COG -->|\"propose\"| STATE\n    WORKER -->|\"request effect\"| EC\n\n    STATE -->|\"identity · epoch · grant · operation\"| EC\n    EC -->|\"authorized dispatch\"| EXT\n    EXT --> OBS\n    OBS -->|\"observed result\"| EVID\n    OBS -->|\"state transition\"| STATE\n\n    EC -. \"deny\" .-> STATE\n```\n\nText equivalent:\n\n```text\nHuman/Org → Regency → authoritative governance state\n                         ↓\n                     VERA identity\n                   ↙              ↘\n             cognition       Continuum Computer\n                                  ↓\n                               worker\n                                  ↓ proposal\n                           Effect Controller\n                       ↙ authority      ↓ dispatch\n             authoritative state   external system\n                                      ↓\n                                observation\n                                ↙          ↘\n                            state        evidence\n```\n\nFor accessibility, every meaningful image should have useful alternative text, and visual information should not be the only representation of important system semantics. GitHub's accessibility guidance emphasizes alt text for images, while WCAG specifies minimum text contrast ratios of 4.5:1 for normal text and 3:1 for large text and generally prefers actual text to images of text. citeturn7search10turn7search5turn7search1\n\n### The DCA presentation model\n\nDo not introduce DCA as a wall of branded names. Lead with responsibilities:\n\n| DCA responsibility | Required question | Continuum mechanism |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Environment** | Where can the principal execute and under which resource/isolation limits? | Continuum Computer, placement, generation |\n| **State** | What is durable and authoritative? | transactional domain store, artifacts, versioned context |\n| **Authority** | Who may do what, against which target, until when? | VERA identity, epochs, grants, approvals |\n| **Execution** | How is untrusted intent transformed into constrained computation? | worker/sandbox, NEXUS direction |\n| **Evidence & recovery** | What happened, what remains uncertain, and how is recovery fenced? | operation state, provider observation, evidence, reconciliation |\n| **Coordination** | How do principals exchange work without merging authority? | tasks, mandates, organizations, handoffs |\n| **Cognition boundary** | How may model outputs influence execution without becoming permission? | model/context/tool bindings and proposal validation |\n\nThen state the cross-cutting invariant:\n\n> **The Effect Boundary is not another subsystem. It is the trust transition between proposed intent and consequential external effect.**\n\n### Capability presentation must encode maturity\n\nUse a capability matrix with explicit status classes:\n\n| Capability | Status | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Persistent VERA identity | `DESIGN` / `IMPLEMENTED` / `VALIDATED` | Link to test/spec |\n| Principal epoch revocation | same | Link |\n| Continuum Computer placement fencing | same | Link |\n| Effect admission | same | Link |\n| Durable operation reservation | same | Link |\n| `UNKNOWN` reconciliation | same | Link |\n| Evidence chain | same | Link |\n| NEXUS sandbox | same | Link |\n| AEON-IQ governed memory | same | Link |\n| Multi-VERA organizations | same | Link |\n\nNever encode status only using color.\n\nUse text labels such as:\n\n```text\nDESIGN\nIMPLEMENTED\nLOCALLY VALIDATED\nEXPERIMENTAL\nQUALIFIED\nBLOCKED\nPLANNED\n```\n\nThat vocabulary is more informative than “beta” for infrastructure with safety-critical semantics.\n\n### Badges and interactive tooling\n\nThe badge row should be evidence-bearing.\n\nRecommended:\n\n| Badge/tool | Purpose | Priority | Notes |\n|---|---|---:|---|\n| GitHub Actions CI badge | Shows current default-branch verification | P0 | First-party, direct |\n| Release/version badge | Indicates latest release | P0 after releases | Prefer canonical release source |\n| License badge | Removes licensing ambiguity | P0 | Link directly to `LICENSE` |\n| OpenSSF Scorecard | Supply-chain posture signal | P1 | Link to detailed results, not only badge |\n| Codecov | Coverage telemetry | P1 | Coverage is not correctness |\n| Codespaces | One-click reproducible developer entry | P1 | Strong DX win |\n| Shields.io endpoint badge | Custom measured project signal | P2 | Only for machine-derived canonical data |\n| Artifact attestation link | Build provenance verification | P1 | More valuable than a cosmetic badge |\n| GitHub Pages demo | Rich interactive architecture / traces | P1 | Better fit for actual interactivity |\n| Zenodo DOI | Archival citation | P2 for papers/releases | Useful for institutional research artifacts |\n\nGitHub Actions officially supports workflow-status badges. Shields.io supports static and dynamic/endpoint-generated badges, Codecov documents README coverage badges, and OpenSSF Scorecard can publish repository security-health results. The OpenSSF badge should remain a pointer to evidence rather than a security guarantee; even badge delivery can experience caching/staleness. citeturn10search3turn5search21turn5search3turn5search2turn5search1turn5search10\n\n### Proposed acceptance rubric for the README itself\n\nTreat README quality as measurable.\n\n| Metric | Target |\n|---|---:|\n| Time to understand system category | `< 15 s` in moderated usability test |\n| Time to locate architecture | `< 20 s` |\n| Time to first runnable success | `< 5 min` from clean supported environment; aspirational `< 60 s` for Codespaces |\n| Major claims with direct evidence links | `100%` |\n| Quickstart commands executed in CI | `100%` |\n| Dead internal links | `0` |\n| Dead external critical links | `0` at scheduled check |\n| Images without meaningful alt text | `0` |\n| Unlabeled roadmap/design claims | `0` |\n| Published benchmark numbers without environment/methodology | `0` |\n| Security guarantees without assumptions | `0` |\n| Release artifacts lacking provenance verification | `0` once public release policy requires attestations |\n\nThese are proposed Continuum quality gates, not GitHub standards.\n\n## Full example README ready for repository adaptation\n\nThe following version deliberately avoids claiming implementation evidence that has not been independently verified here. Replace the single repository slug placeholder, connect status entries to real artifacts, and promote capabilities from `DESIGN` or `EXPERIMENTAL` only when their evidence gates pass.\n\n````markdown\n# Continuum\n### Persistent, governed runtime infrastructure for autonomous principals.\n\n**Asentxia Systems** · **Architecture:** Distributed Cognitive Architecture (DCA) · **Principal:** VERA · **Governance:** The Regency\n\n<!-- Replace ORG/REPO once with the canonical public repository slug. -->\n\n[![CI](https://github.com/ORG/REPO/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg?branch=main)](https://github.com/ORG/REPO/actions/workflows/ci.yml)\n[![Security](https://github.com/ORG/REPO/actions/workflows/security.yml/badge.svg?branch=main)](https://github.com/ORG/REPO/actions/workflows/security.yml)\n[![Open in GitHub Codespaces](https://github.com/codespaces/badge.svg)](https://codespaces.new/ORG/REPO?quickstart=1)\n[![License](https://img.shields.io/badge/license-see%20LICENSE-informational)](./LICENSE)\n\n**[Architecture](#architecture-topology) · [DCA](#dca-machine-native-infrastructure) · [Quickstart](#deployment-and-initialization) · [Security](#security-and-trust-model) · [Evidence](#evidence-and-verification) · [Roadmap](#roadmap)**\n\n> **The principal persists. Components may change.**\n>\n> A model is not an identity. A session is not a lifetime. A tool connection is not permission. An API response is not, by itself, proof of an external outcome.\n\n**Release status:** Research and development. Capabilities in this document are design contracts unless their status links to reproducible qualification evidence.\n\n## Abstract\n\nAutonomous systems are moving beyond bounded inference into long-running work: retaining context and artifacts, operating compute environments, invoking external services, recovering from failure, coordinating other workers, and changing real systems. Persistence alone does not solve the resulting systems problem. Durable work requires explicit ownership of identity, state, authority, execution, uncertainty, recovery, and evidence.\n\n**Continuum** is Asentxia's implementation of **Distributed Cognitive Architecture (DCA)**: an architecture centered on a durable autonomous principal rather than a transient model session or worker process. Its principal, **VERA** — *Verifiable Entity with Revocable Authority* — is separated from the model providing cognition, the Computer providing execution, the credentials expressing machine identity, and the controllers authorized to produce consequential effects. Continuum combines durable state, scoped authority, isolated execution, effect mediation, explicit uncertain outcomes, recovery fencing, inspectable evidence, and governed coordination.\n\nThe design objective is precise:\n\n> **Replace cognition, workers, or placements without silently replacing identity, expanding authority, duplicating effects, or rewriting operational truth.**\n\nContinuum is not a claim that persistence, workflow recovery, agent memory, or multi-agent execution are new concepts. DCA addresses the stricter boundary between **what an autonomous system proposes** and **what an accountable principal is authorized to cause**.\n\n## The operating gap\n\nModern agent runtimes increasingly provide sessions, memory, tools, guardrails, tracing, and durable execution. Those capabilities are necessary. They do not by themselves define one unified answer to the following questions:\n\n| Question | Continuum contract |\n|---|---|\n| Who is accountable for the operation? | A durable VERA principal. |\n| Which state is authoritative? | Explicit transactional domain state; derived memory is not authority. |\n| Which model owns the identity? | None. Cognition is a replaceable binding. |\n| Which worker may emit an effect? | Only a currently eligible placement through the trusted effect path. |\n| What permits the exact effect? | Current scoped grant and, where required, exact approval. |\n| What prevents a stale worker from acting? | Principal/placement generation fencing. |\n| What happens after an ambiguous timeout? | The operation becomes `UNKNOWN`; no blind resend. |\n| Who declares external success? | A qualifying external observation/reconciliation contract, not model self-report. |\n| What can another agent inherit? | Work or context according to policy; authority does not merge by default. |\n| What survives recovery? | Durable identity/state/evidence; effect capability is re-established only through current eligibility. |\n\n## Core topology\n\nContinuum decomposes autonomous operation into explicit trust and ownership boundaries:\n\n- **VERA** — durable autonomous principal and accountability root.\n- **Continuum Computer** — logical execution environment with independent placement generations.\n- **Cognition Gateway** — replaceable model/context/tool binding that may propose work but does not grant itself authority.\n- **Authoritative State** — transactional identity, tasks, grants, approvals, operations, artifacts, and evidence.\n- **Effect Controller** — independent credential-bearing enforcement boundary for consequential external actions.\n- **Evidence and Reconciliation Plane** — records what was attempted, observed, resolved, or remains unknown.\n- **The Regency** — governance over organizations, mandates, roles, approvals, and lifecycle.\n- **Focus Desk** — human operating surface for work, approval, evidence, incidents, and recovery.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TB\n    HUMAN[\"Human / Organization\"]\n    REG[\"The Regency<br/>governance · mandates · approvals\"]\n\n    subgraph TRUST[\"Continuum trusted domain\"]\n        STATE[\"Authoritative State<br/>identity · grants · tasks · operations · evidence\"]\n        ID[\"VERA Identity<br/>principal · epoch\"]\n        EC[\"Effect Controller<br/>admission · fencing · provider credentials\"]\n        OBS[\"Observation / Reconciliation\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph ENV[\"Continuum Computer\"]\n        COG[\"Cognition Gateway<br/>model · context · planning\"]\n        WORK[\"Worker / NEXUS<br/>constrained execution\"]\n    end\n\n    EXT[\"External Systems\"]\n\n    HUMAN --> REG\n    REG --> STATE\n    STATE --> ID\n\n    ID --> COG\n    COG -->|\"proposal\"| STATE\n    STATE --> WORK\n\n    WORK -->|\"effect request\"| EC\n    STATE -->|\"current authority + immutable operation\"| EC\n\n    EC -->|\"authorized dispatch\"| EXT\n    EXT --> OBS\n    OBS --> STATE\n```\n\n### Data flow\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant R as Regency / Owner\n    participant V as VERA\n    participant S as Authoritative State\n    participant E as Effect Controller\n    participant P as External Provider\n\n    R->>S: Establish mandate / grant\n    V->>S: Propose immutable operation\n    S-->>V: operation_id + admitted binding\n\n    V->>E: Request effect(operation_id)\n    E->>S: Load current principal, epoch, placement, grant, approval\n\n    alt Authority invalid or stale\n        E-->>V: DENIED\n    else Authority valid\n        E->>S: Reserve operation before external I/O\n        E->>P: Dispatch exact bound operation\n\n        alt Authoritative matching observation\n            P-->>E: Observed outcome\n            E->>S: COMMITTED + evidence\n        else Ambiguous timeout / incomplete observation\n            E->>S: UNKNOWN\n            Note over E,P: Do not blindly resend\n            E->>P: Read-only reconciliation\n            P-->>E: Matching / absent / unresolved observation\n            E->>S: Reconcile or preserve UNKNOWN\n        end\n    end\n```\n\n### State ownership\n\n```text\nCognition owns proposals.\nWorkers own ephemeral execution.\nVERA owns accountable continuity.\nAuthoritative state owns durable domain truth.\nThe Effect Controller owns final effect admission.\nExternal providers own external truth.\nEvidence records the relationship between those domains.\n```\n\n## DCA machine-native infrastructure\n\nDCA defines seven architectural responsibilities. The **Effect Boundary** crosses them at the transition from proposed intent to consequential external effect.\n\n| Responsibility | Required contract | Continuum mechanism |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Environment** | Execution locus, resources, isolation, lifecycle | Continuum Computer, placements, provider adapters |\n| **State** | Durable ownership and authoritative truth | transactional domain store, artifacts, versioned context |\n| **Authority** | Actor, target, action, scope, expiry, revocation | VERA identity, epochs, grants, approvals |\n| **Execution** | Constrained transformation of intent into computation | workers, sandboxing, NEXUS direction |\n| **Evidence & Recovery** | Outcome observation, uncertainty, fencing, restoration | evidence records, `UNKNOWN`, reconciliation, generations |\n| **Coordination** | Work exchange without implicit authority merging | tasks, mandates, organizations, handoffs |\n| **Cognition Boundary** | Model flexibility without authority coupling | model/context/tool bindings and proposal validation |\n\n> **Invariant:** the Effect Boundary is not bypassed because a model, worker, scheduler, dashboard, memory service, or integration can describe or request an action.\n\n### VERA\n\nA **VERA** is a durable accountable principal whose identity is distinct from:\n\n```text\nmodel identity\nworker identity\nprocess identity\nmachine identity\nplacement identity\ncredential identity\nconversation identity\n```\n\nReplacing any one of those components does not, by itself, create a new VERA or expand the existing VERA's authority.\n\n### Continuum Computer\n\nA **Continuum Computer** is the logical environment assigned to authorized work.\n\n```text\nComputer ID           = durable logical resource\nPlacement ID          = current physical/runtime realization\nPlacement generation  = fencing version\nWorker                = ephemeral executor within a placement\n```\n\nA recovered old placement must not regain effect capability merely because it still has local state.\n\n### The Regency\n\nThe Regency controls institutional governance:\n\n```text\norganizations\nmemberships\nroles\nmandates\napprovals\nprincipal lifecycle\nrecovery decisions\npolicy\n```\n\nA role is not automatically a grant.  \nA mandate is not automatically a grant.  \nAn approval is not an external effect.\n\n### Effect Controller\n\nThe Effect Controller is the trusted transition from **proposed** to **permitted-to-dispatch**.\n\nIt validates the exact operation against current:\n\n```text\nprincipal identity\nprincipal epoch\nComputer / placement generation\ntarget\naction\nparameters / digest\ngrant\napproval requirement\npolicy revision\ndeadline / expiry\noperation identity\n```\n\nProvider-wide credentials belong at this enforcement boundary, not inside unrestricted model or worker processes.\n\n### NEXUS\n\n**NEXUS** is the planned capability-oriented constrained execution layer for machine-generated computation.\n\nQualification requires explicit bounds on:\n\n```text\nmodule provenance\nfilesystem reachability\nnetwork reachability\ncredential reachability\nmemory\nCPU / fuel\nwall-clock time\noutput size\nsyscall surface\ncancellation\n```\n\nNEXUS isolation is not considered qualified merely because a module compiles to WebAssembly.\n\n### AEON-IQ\n\n**AEON-IQ** is the direction for governed derived memory and contextual retrieval.\n\nIts core trust rule is:\n\n> **Retrieved information may inform a proposal. Retrieval does not grant authority.**\n\nDerived memory remains distinct from:\n\n```text\nidentity\ngrants\napprovals\nauthoritative task state\neffect state\nexternally observed truth\n```\n\n## Capabilities\n\n| Capability | Architectural result | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| Durable principal identity | Accountability survives model/process replacement | `DESIGN — attach evidence before promotion` |\n| Revocable principal epochs | Stale credentials/placements can be fenced | `DESIGN` |\n| Logical Continuum Computers | Environment identity separated from placement | `DESIGN` |\n| Scoped authority | Permission narrowed to actor/action/target/version/time | `DESIGN` |\n| Independent effect admission | Cognition cannot self-authorize consequential effects | `DESIGN` |\n| Immutable operation binding | Intent cannot mutate silently after admission | `DESIGN` |\n| Reserve-before-send | Internal sender ownership established before provider I/O | `DESIGN` |\n| Explicit `UNKNOWN` | Ambiguous outcomes remain unresolved instead of being guessed | `DESIGN` |\n| Provider reconciliation | Outcome can be resolved from authoritative external observation | `DESIGN` |\n| Evidence records | Agent narrative separated from system observations | `DESIGN` |\n| Placement fencing | Stale recovered workers remain ineligible | `DESIGN` |\n| Governed memory | Derived knowledge cannot become implicit permission | `PLANNED` |\n| NEXUS constrained execution | Generated code runs with bounded capabilities | `PLANNED` |\n| Multi-VERA organizations | Coordinated principals retain separate accountability | `PLANNED` |\n| Federated interoperability | Principals exchange structured work across trust domains | `RESEARCH` |\n\nReplace each status with a link to its qualification artifact when implementation evidence exists.\n\n## What this enables\n\n### Long-horizon work without session identity\n\nA VERA can continue responsibility for a task while cognition, processes, or placements are replaced.\n\n### Model substitution without implicit privilege escalation\n\nA stronger model may be bound to an existing principal without automatically receiving broader external-effect authority.\n\n### Recoverable autonomous infrastructure\n\nRecovery can restore durable work while rotating/fencing the execution capability that might otherwise create duplicate effects.\n\n### Explicit operational uncertainty\n\nNetwork ambiguity becomes a represented state requiring reconciliation rather than an excuse for unsafe automatic replay.\n\n### Evidence-driven autonomy\n\nThe system can distinguish:\n\n```text\nproposed\nadmitted\nauthorized\nreserved\ndispatched\nobserved\ncommitted\ndenied\nfailed\nunknown\nreconciled\n```\n\n### Governed autonomous organizations\n\nMultiple principals can exchange work under explicit mandates while preserving separate identities and scoped authority.\n\n### Machine-native control paths\n\nWhere a structured machine interface exists, autonomous systems can use typed/versioned operations rather than unnecessary human-interface emulation while retaining authorization and audit boundaries.\n\n## Deployment and initialization\n\n### Repository\n\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/ORG/REPO.git\ncd REPO\n```\n\n### Reproducible development environment\n\nPreferred:\n\n```bash\ngh codespace create --repo ORG/REPO\n```\n\nOr open the repository through the **Open in GitHub Codespaces** badge.\n\nLocal environments should consume the checked-in development-container definition:\n\n```text\n.devcontainer/devcontainer.json\n```\n\n### Native development\n\nThe canonical repository must expose one supported bootstrap interface:\n\n```bash\nmake bootstrap\nmake check\nmake test\nmake run\n```\n\nThe names above are the target developer contract. Do not publish them until they execute successfully in CI and from a clean supported environment.\n\n### Production initialization\n\nProduction deployment must not be represented as supported until the following gates are qualified:\n\n```text\n[ ] supported release artifact\n[ ] immutable dependency lock\n[ ] migration contract\n[ ] backup/restore qualification\n[ ] placement fencing test\n[ ] external provider reconciliation test\n[ ] secret-management integration\n[ ] threat-model review\n[ ] rollback / safe-resume procedure\n[ ] release provenance verification\n```\n\n## Performance invariants and mathematical guarantees\n\nContinuum separates **semantic invariants**, **measured performance**, and **targets**.\n\n### Authority invariant\n\nFor principal \\(p\\), operation \\(o\\), and time \\(t\\):\n\n\\[\nDispatch(o,t)\n\\Rightarrow\nValidPrincipal(p,t)\n\\land ValidEpoch(p,t)\n\\land ValidPlacement(o,t)\n\\land ValidGrant(p,o,t)\n\\land PolicyAllows(o,t)\n\\]\n\nIf an approval is required:\n\n\\[\nDispatch(o,t) \\Rightarrow ValidApproval(o,t)\n\\]\n\nA cognition replacement alone must not increase the principal's effect-authority set:\n\n\\[\nA_{after}(p) \\subseteq A_{before}(p)\n\\]\n\nunless a separate valid authority transition explicitly expands that set.\n\n### Sender uniqueness\n\nFor one immutable operation identity:\n\n\\[\n\\left|ActiveInternalSender(o)\\right| \\le 1\n\\]\n\nThis is an internal Continuum property to be tested under the documented transaction/fencing assumptions. It is **not** a claim of exactly-once behavior across arbitrary external providers.\n\n### Ambiguous-outcome invariant\n\nIf provider I/O may have occurred but no qualifying outcome can be established:\n\n\\[\nAttempted(o) \\land \\neg AuthoritativeOutcome(o)\n\\Rightarrow State(o)=UNKNOWN\n\\]\n\nGeneric automatic resend is prohibited while the unresolved operation could already have taken effect.\n\n### Placement-fencing invariant\n\nFor any effect-emitting request:\n\n\\[\ngeneration_{request}=generation_{current}\n\\]\n\nis a necessary condition for admission.\n\n### Latency model\n\nMeasure effect latency as:\n\n\\[\nT_{effect} =\nT_{admission}\n+T_{authority}\n+T_{reserve}\n+T_{provider}\n+T_{observe}\n+T_{commit}\n\\]\n\nReport at minimum:\n\n```text\np50\np95\np99\nmax under bounded benchmark interval\nthroughput\nCPU / operation\nallocations / operation\nbytes written / operation\nnetwork bytes / operation\n```\n\n### Autonomy overhead\n\nTreat autonomy overhead as a measurement, not a theorem:\n\n\\[\nOverhead_{relative}\n=\n\\frac{T_{Continuum}-T_{baseline}}\n     {T_{baseline}}\n\\]\n\nThe baseline must perform the same external operation under an explicitly documented comparison boundary.\n\nNo sub-millisecond, throughput, availability, or overhead guarantee is published until a reproducible benchmark artifact demonstrates it under stated hardware and workload.\n\n## Security and trust model\n\n### Trust boundary\n\n**Trusted for effect integrity:**\n\n```text\nauthoritative database / transaction semantics\nidentity and authority verifier\nEffect Controller\nverification keys / credential issuance\nprovider adapter used for observation\nrelease and migration procedures inside the declared TCB\n```\n\n**Not trusted to self-authorize:**\n\n```text\nfoundation model output\nretrieved content\nweb content\nworker-generated code\nordinary UI clients\nscheduler intent\nderived memory\nexternal instructions\n```\n\n### Threats Continuum must test\n\n```text\nprompt injection\ncredential leakage\ngrant replay\napproval replay\nprincipal-epoch replay\nstale placement\nduplicate delivery\nrequest reordering\nnetwork partitions\nworker crash before / during / after provider I/O\ndatabase failover\npartial restore\nmalicious retrieved content\ncross-tenant state access\ncorrupt artifact\nforged evidence\noperator mistake\nprovider inconsistency\n```\n\n### Non-guarantees\n\nA cryptographic signature can establish origin/integrity under its key assumptions; it does not establish that the signed statement is semantically true.\n\nA hash chain can make changes detectable under its assumptions; it does not make privileged infrastructure physically incapable of deleting the entire chain.\n\nA successful model response does not establish an external effect.\n\nA timeout does not establish that an external effect did not happen.\n\nA test suite does not prove absence of all security defects.\n\nA sandbox is not an authority system unless credentials and external effect paths are constrained outside the sandbox.\n\n## Evidence and verification\n\nEvery major capability should terminate in an artifact.\n\n| Claim | Required evidence |\n|---|---|\n| VERA survives process replacement | restart/reconstitution test |\n| Revocation prevents stale operation | epoch-replay test |\n| Old placement cannot emit | placement-fencing fault test |\n| Duplicate request does not create duplicate internal sender | concurrency/property test |\n| Ambiguous send does not blindly replay | fault-injection test |\n| Provider observation determines commitment | reconciliation integration test |\n| Model cannot bypass authority | adversarial integration test |\n| Memory cannot grant authority | tainted-context security test |\n| Tenant state remains isolated | cross-tenant property/integration test |\n| Backup/restore is safe | destructive recovery qualification |\n| Published benchmark is reproducible | benchmark harness + raw data + hardware metadata |\n| Release originates from CI | artifact attestation / provenance |\n\nRecommended evidence tree:\n\n```text\nevidence/\n├── qualification/\n│   ├── identity/\n│   ├── authority/\n│   ├── effects/\n│   ├── recovery/\n│   └── isolation/\n├── benchmarks/\n│   ├── methodology.md\n│   ├── environments/\n│   └── results/\n├── threat-model/\n├── attestations/\n└── releases/\n```\n\n> **Evidence terminates the claim.**\n\n## Developer experience\n\nThe repository should provide one canonical path from clone to validated local state.\n\n```text\nclone\n  ↓\ndevcontainer / bootstrap\n  ↓\ndependency integrity check\n  ↓\ndatabase / fixture initialization\n  ↓\nunit tests\n  ↓\nintegration tests\n  ↓\nfault tests\n  ↓\nlocal Continuum runtime\n  ↓\nexample operation\n  ↓\ninspect evidence\n```\n\nRequired developer commands:\n\n```bash\nmake bootstrap      # establish supported development environment\nmake check          # format + lint + static analysis\nmake test           # deterministic unit/integration suite\nmake test-faults    # crash/retry/recovery/fencing suite\nmake bench          # reproducible benchmark harness\nmake demo           # deterministic local demonstration\nmake docs           # build / validate documentation\nmake verify-release # verify provenance/signatures for selected artifact\n```\n\nThese commands become documentation only after CI executes the same paths.\n\n## Repository structure\n\n```text\n.\n├── README.md\n├── LICENSE\n├── SECURITY.md\n├── CONTRIBUTING.md\n├── CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md\n├── CITATION.cff\n├── CHANGELOG.md\n├── .devcontainer/\n├── .github/\n│   ├── workflows/\n│   ├── ISSUE_TEMPLATE/\n│   └── PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md\n├── docs/\n│   ├── architecture/\n│   ├── protocols/\n│   ├── security/\n│   ├── operations/\n│   ├── benchmarks/\n│   └── adr/\n├── evidence/\n├── examples/\n├── scripts/\n└── src/\n```\n\n## Roadmap\n\nRoadmap dates are planning targets, not availability guarantees.\n\n```mermaid\ngantt\n    title Continuum proposed qualification roadmap\n    dateFormat  YYYY-MM-DD\n\n    section Foundation\n    Evidence contract and repo front page     :2026-10-15, 45d\n    Reproducible developer environment        :2026-10-15, 60d\n    Threat model and benchmark harness        :2026-11-01, 60d\n\n    section DCA Core\n    Identity and authority contracts          :2027-01-01, 90d\n    Effect Controller qualification           :2027-01-15, 75d\n    UNKNOWN and recovery qualification        :2027-02-01, 60d\n\n    section Machine Infrastructure\n    Continuum Computer recovery               :2027-04-01, 90d\n    NEXUS constrained execution               :2027-04-15, 75d\n    AEON-IQ governed memory integration        :2027-05-01, 60d\n\n    section Governance\n    Regency and Focus Desk                    :2027-07-01, 90d\n    Multi-VERA coordination                   :2027-07-15, 75d\n    External security assessment              :2027-08-01, 60d\n\n    section Federation\n    Inter-runtime protocol preview            :2027-10-01, 75d\n    VERA Organizations alpha                  :2027-10-15, 60d\n    Public qualification bundle               :2027-11-01, 60d\n```\n\n### Qualification gates\n\n**Foundation**\n- Canonical schemas and terminology.\n- CI-enforced quickstart.\n- Threat model.\n- Benchmark methodology.\n- Evidence taxonomy.\n\n**DCA Core**\n- VERA lifecycle.\n- Principal epochs.\n- Scoped grants.\n- Effect admission.\n- Durable operation binding.\n- Explicit `UNKNOWN`.\n- Provider reconciliation.\n\n**Machine Infrastructure**\n- Continuum Computer lifecycle.\n- Placement generation and recovery.\n- NEXUS constrained execution.\n- Governed derived memory.\n- Backup/restore qualification.\n\n**Governance**\n- Regency policy model.\n- Focus Desk.\n- Governed handoffs.\n- Multi-principal organizations.\n- Incident/recovery operator flows.\n\n**Federation**\n- Versioned machine-to-machine protocol.\n- Cross-runtime identity and authority exchange.\n- Replay protection.\n- Portable evidence.\n- Organization/collective research.\n\n## Contributing, security, citation, and license\n\nRead:\n\n- [`CONTRIBUTING.md`](./CONTRIBUTING.md) before submitting changes.\n- [`SECURITY.md`](./SECURITY.md) for private vulnerability reporting.\n- [`CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md`](./CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md) for community expectations.\n- [`CITATION.cff`](./CITATION.cff) for research citation metadata.\n- [`LICENSE`](./LICENSE) for license terms.\n\nArchitectural changes that alter identity, authority, operation semantics, evidence, recovery, or trust boundaries require an ADR under `docs/adr/`.\n\n## Project doctrine\n\n```text\nIdentity is not a session.\nMemory is not authority.\nAvailability is not permission.\nA role is not a grant.\nAn approval is not an effect.\nA request is not an outcome.\nA timeout is not a rollback.\nA signature is not truth.\nA model is not the principal.\nRecovery must not resurrect stale authority.\n```\n\n**The principal persists. The components may change.**\n````\n\nThat README is intentionally stricter than conventional product copy. Its authority comes from preserving distinctions that distributed systems and security engineering actually require.\n\n## Repository implementation, verification, and governance\n\n### Recommended repository-level files\n\nGitHub's community-health model recognizes files including `README`, `LICENSE`, `CONTRIBUTING`, and `CODE_OF_CONDUCT`; GitHub also provides native handling for security policies and citation files. `CITATION.cff` gives repositories structured citation metadata, while `SECURITY.md` creates an explicit vulnerability-reporting path. citeturn8search19turn8search1turn8search2turn8search15turn8search7\n\nCreate this minimum surface:\n\n```bash\nmkdir -p \\\n  .github/workflows \\\n  .github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE \\\n  .devcontainer \\\n  docs/{architecture,protocols,security,operations,benchmarks,adr} \\\n  evidence/{qualification,benchmarks,attestations,releases} \\\n  examples \\\n  assets\n\ntouch \\\n  SECURITY.md \\\n  CONTRIBUTING.md \\\n  CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md \\\n  CITATION.cff \\\n  CHANGELOG.md \\\n  docs/architecture/overview.md \\\n  docs/security/threat-model.md \\\n  docs/benchmarks/methodology.md\n```\n\nThe exact content must match the actual licensing, disclosure, contribution, and architecture policies; empty institutional files are worse than no claim of completeness.\n\n### Developer-environment path\n\nUse `.devcontainer/devcontainer.json` as the canonical machine-readable development environment when Codespaces is supported. GitHub documents dev containers as a way to define tools, runtimes, extensions, settings, and ports for Codespaces. citeturn0search1turn10search8\n\nThe README can then expose:\n\n```markdown\n[![Open in GitHub Codespaces](https://github.com/codespaces/badge.svg)](\n  https://codespaces.new/ORG/REPO?quickstart=1\n)\n```\n\nGitHub officially documents this badge/deep-link workflow. citeturn10search0\n\n### CI architecture\n\nA serious Continuum repository should have independent jobs for:\n\n```text\nCI\n├── build\n├── format / lint\n├── unit\n├── integration\n├── concurrency / property\n├── fault injection\n├── migration\n├── backup / restore\n├── security\n├── dependency review\n├── documentation\n├── quickstart replay\n├── benchmark smoke test\n└── release provenance\n```\n\nGitHub Actions is designed to automate build, test, and deployment workflows, and workflow status can be surfaced directly as a README badge. GitHub also supports job summaries through `GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY`, which is useful for generating compact benchmark, test, or qualification summaries directly from CI rather than manually copying them into documentation. citeturn10search10turn10search3turn10search2\n\nA key pattern should be:\n\n```text\nREADME command\n     │\n     └── must call same script/target\n             │\n             └── executed by CI\n```\n\nDo not maintain a “README installation path” and a separate “real CI path.” That creates documentation drift.\n\n### Evidence-bearing supply-chain infrastructure\n\nGitHub artifact attestations can attach signed provenance claims to build outputs and later verify where/how an artifact was produced. GitHub documents `gh attestation verify` for consumer-side verification and supports provenance patterns aligned with higher SLSA build levels. citeturn6search0turn6search4turn6search12\n\nA publication flow should become:\n\n```text\nsource commit\n    ↓\nprotected CI workflow\n    ↓\ntests + security gates\n    ↓\nbuild artifact\n    ↓\nartifact attestation\n    ↓\nrelease\n    ↓\nconsumer verifies provenance\n```\n\nExample verification shape:\n\n```bash\ngh attestation verify ./continuum-artifact \\\n  --repo ORG/REPO\n```\n\nThe current SLSA framework defines build-provenance requirements around properties such as authenticity, completeness, and accuracy; those concepts are useful for Continuum releases because they make “this binary came from this repository/workflow” a separately verifiable statement. citeturn6search9turn6search17\n\nFor OCI/container or broader artifact ecosystems, Sigstore Cosign can verify signatures and in-toto attestations and supports identity-based/keyless signing models. citeturn6search2turn6search6turn6search22\n\n### Dependency and repository security\n\nEnable dependency visibility and PR-level dependency review. GitHub's dependency-review tooling exposes dependency changes and known vulnerabilities in pull requests and can be configured as a merge gate; Dependabot can automatically create security-update pull requests for vulnerable dependencies, including GitHub Actions dependencies. citeturn6search7turn6search35turn6search3\n\nAlso configure secret scanning/push protection where repository/account support allows it, and maintain an explicit security reporting policy. citeturn8search9turn8search25\n\nFor release transparency, GitHub can generate SPDX-compatible SBOM information from the dependency graph. That should be published or linked when releases become externally consumed. citeturn8search28\n\n### Interactive experience\n\nA README cannot itself become a full browser application without fighting GitHub's sanitization model. The optimal architecture is:\n\n```text\nREADME\n  │\n  ├── Native Mermaid topology\n  ├── Animated GIF/video thumbnail only when explanatory\n  ├── Codespaces → live runnable Continuum\n  ├── Pages → interactive DCA explorer\n  ├── Pages → benchmark dashboard\n  └── Pages → evidence/replay visualizer\n```\n\nGitHub Pages is designed to host HTML/CSS/JavaScript static sites and can be deployed through Actions, making it a better location for an interactive graph or trace visualizer than attempting unsupported README embeds. citeturn10search17turn10search5turn10search13\n\nA useful interactive DCA explorer should let the reader select an operation and inspect:\n\n```text\nPrincipal\n  VERA ID\n  epoch\n  organization\n  mandate\n\nEnvironment\n  Computer ID\n  placement\n  generation\n\nAuthority\n  grant\n  approval\n  policy version\n  expiry\n\nOperation\n  operation_id\n  canonical parameter digest\n  state transition history\n\nExternal result\n  dispatch attempt\n  provider identifier\n  observation\n  reconciliation\n\nEvidence\n  trace\n  signatures / digests\n  attestation\n  linked tests\n```\n\nThat would be genuinely interactive and technically meaningful rather than decorative.\n\n### Badge policy\n\nKeep the first badge row to roughly five or six items:\n\n```text\nCI | Security | Release | Coverage | License | Codespaces\n```\n\nAvoid:\n\n```text\nstars\nfork counts\nvisitor counters\n\"awesome\" badges\nlarge stacks of framework logos\nunverified \"production ready\" badges\narbitrary AI-generated quality scores\n```\n\nA custom Shields endpoint is appropriate for a real Continuum qualification metric only when the value comes from a canonical machine-readable artifact. Shields supports endpoint badges backed by JSON, but the authoritative information should remain the underlying artifact rather than the rendered pixel. citeturn5search3turn5search21\n\nA stronger custom badge than “build passing” might eventually be:\n\n```text\nDCA Qualification: 47 / 52 gates\n```\n\nbut only if clicking it opens the exact qualification manifest showing every passed, failed, blocked, and not-run gate.\n\n### Accessibility checklist\n\nThe publication gate should require:\n\n- Meaningful alt text for every meaningful image.\n- Text equivalents for critical architecture diagrams.\n- No state communicated by color alone.\n- Adequate foreground/background contrast.\n- Avoid rasterized paragraphs or code as images.\n- Keep headings hierarchical.\n- Use descriptive link labels rather than repeated “click here.”\n- Avoid autoplaying visual content.\n- Provide a reduced-motion/static alternative for animations when necessary.\n- Ensure the interactive Pages application is keyboard operable and uses semantic controls.\n- Test light and dark GitHub rendering.\n\nGitHub specifically recommends useful alternative text for images, while WCAG provides the governing contrast and text-equivalent principles. citeturn7search2turn7search10turn7search5turn7search1\n\n### Legal and disclosure checklist\n\nThis is repository-governance guidance, not legal advice.\n\nBefore institutional publication, resolve:\n\n| Item | Publication question |\n|---|---|\n| **License** | Is redistribution/use/modification actually permitted under an explicit license? |\n| **Copyright** | Is ownership accurate for code, diagrams, papers, and generated assets? |\n| **Third-party notices** | Do bundled dependencies/assets require notices or attribution? |\n| **Trademark** | Are Asentxia, Continuum, VERA, DCA, Regency, NEXUS, AEON-IQ names governed consistently? |\n| **Security disclosure** | Does `SECURITY.md` provide a non-public vulnerability path? |\n| **Privacy** | Does telemetry, tracing, memory, or evidence storage collect personal/sensitive information? |\n| **AI-provider terms** | Are model/API integrations described consistently with their contractual restrictions? |\n| **Dataset/model licensing** | Do any bundled weights, datasets, evaluation sets, or examples have separate terms? |\n| **Cryptography/export** | Does deployment geography or cryptographic distribution require specialist review? |\n| **Benchmark disclosure** | Are comparison methods and environments disclosed sufficiently to avoid misleading claims? |\n| **Research citation** | Are papers, datasets, and external architectures properly attributed? |\n| **Patent review** | Do strong novelty claims or protocol publications require IP review before disclosure? |\n\nGitHub recommends an explicit license because, absent one, default copyright rules apply rather than an inferred open-source permission. GitHub also supports structured citation metadata through `CITATION.cff`. citeturn8search0turn8search2\n\nFor archival research releases, Zenodo can archive GitHub releases and assign DOIs, which is useful if DCA specifications, benchmark releases, or papers need durable scholarly citation. citeturn10search34\n\n## Prioritized roadmap and reference set\n\n### Proposed roadmap from October 2026\n\nThese dates are recommended planning targets based on the information available here; they are **not estimates derived from an inspected current codebase, staffing plan, or measured engineering velocity**. Move dates when actual dependency and implementation status are known.\n\n| Period | Milestone | Required deliverables | Exit criterion |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Oct–Dec 2026** | Repository authority surface | canonical terminology, rebuilt README, `/docs`, `SECURITY`, `CONTRIBUTING`, `CITATION`, threat-model skeleton, devcontainer, Codespaces, CI, link/quickstart validation | every public claim classified as evidence/design/roadmap |\n| **Oct–Dec 2026** | Evidence contract | qualification manifest, claim→test links, evidence directory, benchmark specification | zero major claims without evidence slot |\n| **Nov–Dec 2026** | Baseline benchmark harness | direct baseline vs Continuum, latency decomposition, CPU/memory/storage accounting | reproducible raw results on named hardware |\n| **Jan–Mar 2027** | DCA core contract stabilization | VERA lifecycle, epochs, grants, operations, placements, state-machine specs | schemas versioned; invariants tested |\n| **Jan–Mar 2027** | Effect Controller qualification | reserve-before-send, immutable operation binding, denial semantics, provider adapters | duplicate/reorder/crash tests pass |\n| **Feb–Mar 2027** | Recovery / `UNKNOWN` qualification | ambiguity injection, read-back reconciliation, stale-placement fencing | no blind duplicate dispatch in qualified scenarios |\n| **Jan–Mar 2027** | Attested CI release path | dependency review, provenance, signed/attested build flow, verification docs | released artifact independently verifiable |\n| **Apr–Jun 2027** | Continuum Computer | provider abstraction, placement lifecycle, resource bounds, backup/restore | loss/replacement recovery suite passes |\n| **Apr–Jun 2027** | NEXUS constrained compute | WASM capability contract, filesystem/network/credential isolation, resource limits | adversarial escape/reachability suite meets defined acceptance criteria |\n| **May–Jun 2027** | AEON-IQ governed memory | provenance, quarantine, versioning, erasure/invalidation, authority separation | memory cannot alter authority path |\n| **Jun 2027** | Public performance dossier | p50/p95/p99, throughput, resource model, baseline, raw data | README performance claims fully reproducible |\n| **Jul–Sep 2027** | Regency / Focus Desk | approval/recovery/incidents/work UI, role/mandate/grant differentiation | UI state is server-derived and authorization-safe |\n| **Jul–Sep 2027** | Multi-VERA coordination | handoff protocol, separate authorities, organization invariants | coordination cannot silently union permissions |\n| **Aug–Sep 2027** | External security review | threat-model review, penetration/adversarial testing, remediation | critical/high findings resolved or explicitly blocking |\n| **Oct–Dec 2027** | Federated protocol preview | cross-runtime identity, replay protection, evidence exchange, protocol versions | interoperability demo across independently deployed domains |\n| **Oct–Dec 2027** | VERA Organizations alpha | memberships, mandates, governance lifecycle, bounded delegation research | published safety/authority contract |\n| **Nov–Dec 2027** | Qualification bundle | reproducible demo, benchmark dossier, SBOM, provenance, security report, technical paper | evidence-backed public beta/research-release decision |\n\nThe sequencing deliberately qualifies the authority/effect/recovery core **before** adding sophisticated collective autonomy. That prevents multi-agent coordination from multiplying unresolved authority and recovery semantics.\n\n### Critical roadmap gates\n\nUse gates rather than feature-completion percentages.\n\n**Publication Gate**\n\n```text\nPASS only if:\nREADME quickstart passes from clean environment\nAND all major claims carry status\nAND critical links resolve\nAND license/security/contribution policy exists\nAND architecture terminology is canonical\n```\n\n**DCA Core Gate**\n\n```text\nPASS only if:\nidentity semantics pass\nAND epoch revocation passes\nAND grant evaluation passes\nAND operation immutability passes\nAND placement fencing passes\n```\n\n**External Effect Gate**\n\n```text\nPASS only if:\nreserve-before-send passes\nAND duplicate request tests pass\nAND timeout ambiguity becomes UNKNOWN\nAND blind resend is impossible in qualified paths\nAND provider reconciliation is independently exercised\n```\n\n**Recovery Gate**\n\n```text\nPASS only if:\ncrash-before-send\nAND crash-during-send\nAND crash-after-send\nAND state-store restart\nAND worker resurrection\nAND stale placement\nAND restore-from-backup\n\nall resolve according to the published state machine.\n```\n\n**Production Qualification Gate**\n\n```text\nPASS only if:\nfunctional qualification\nAND security qualification\nAND recovery qualification\nAND release provenance\nAND operational rollback\nAND documented limits\nAND measured capacity envelope\n\nall pass on the intended deployment class.\n```\n\n### Repository-content priority checklist\n\n**P0 — required before the front page should be called institutional-grade**\n\n- Exact one-line technical classification.\n- Two-paragraph abstract.\n- Current maturity/status.\n- Problems and residual industry gap.\n- DCA architecture map.\n- DCA component ownership table.\n- Effect-flow sequence diagram.\n- Quickstart that runs in CI.\n- Security/trust boundary.\n- Explicit non-guarantees.\n- Capability status/evidence matrix.\n- Performance methodology with no unsupported numbers.\n- Roadmap with dates and gates.\n- `LICENSE`.\n- `SECURITY.md`.\n- `CONTRIBUTING.md`.\n- `CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md`.\n- CI badge.\n- Architecture/security/evidence links.\n- Accessible diagrams.\n- Claim-evidence audit.\n\n**P1 — highest-value upgrades**\n\n- Codespaces.\n- `.devcontainer`.\n- GitHub Pages DCA explorer.\n- Public benchmark dashboard.\n- OpenSSF Scorecard.\n- Codecov or equivalent coverage telemetry.\n- Dependency review.\n- Dependabot.\n- Secret scanning/push protection.\n- Artifact attestations.\n- SBOM.\n- `CITATION.cff`.\n- ADR system.\n- Release verification instructions.\n- Automated README quickstart replay.\n- Automated link validation.\n- External threat-model review.\n\n**P2 — add after architecture and evidence stabilize**\n\n- Research paper / DCA specification.\n- Zenodo DOI.\n- Light/dark custom architecture art.\n- Interactive evidence viewer.\n- Public compatibility matrix.\n- Multi-language READMEs.\n- Formal protocol specification.\n- External interoperability demo.\n- Published benchmark history across releases.\n\n### Prioritized primary references\n\n**GitHub repository documentation**\n\n[GitHub — About READMEs](https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/managing-your-repositorys-settings-and-features/customizing-your-repository/about-readmes) explains README placement and the core information GitHub recommends surfacing. citeturn1search2\n\n[GitHub — Creating diagrams](https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/writing-on-github/working-with-advanced-formatting/creating-diagrams) documents native Mermaid rendering. citeturn1search0\n\n[GitHub-Flavored Markdown specification](https://github.github.com/gfm/) defines Markdown behavior including sanitized/disallowed raw HTML. citeturn11search0\n\n[GitHub Codespaces documentation](https://docs.github.com/en/codespaces) and GitHub's Codespaces badge guidance provide the primary path to one-click executable onboarding. citeturn10search0turn0search1\n\n[GitHub Actions documentation](https://docs.github.com/en/actions) defines workflow automation and workflow-status presentation. citeturn10search10turn10search3\n\n[GitHub Pages documentation](https://docs.github.com/en/pages) is the preferred GitHub-native home for a richer interactive DCA explorer or benchmark visualization. citeturn10search17\n\n**Repository exemplars**\n\n[vLLM](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm) is a strong example of concise definition, capability scanning, research links, and rapid activation. citeturn15search0\n\n[PyTorch](https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch) demonstrates the institutional long-form pattern: crisp technical definition followed by extensive build, resource, ecosystem, and contribution information. citeturn15search1\n\n[Hugging Face Transformers](https://github.com/huggingface/transformers) demonstrates fast positioning and practical developer activation across a large ML ecosystem. citeturn15search2\n\n[Google DeepMind AlphaFold](https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphafold) is especially relevant to Continuum's intended research posture because it connects implementation scope to scientific artifacts while stating maturity and platform constraints. citeturn15search3\n\n**Current agent-system baseline**\n\n[OpenAI Agents SDK](https://openai.github.io/openai-agents-python/) establishes that modern agent runtimes already encompass tool execution, guardrails, handoffs, sessions, and broader runtime behavior. citeturn13search1\n\n[OpenAI Agents SDK — Tracing](https://openai.github.io/openai-agents-python/tracing/) is useful prior art for execution observability. citeturn13search7\n\n[Google ADK — Sessions, State and Memory](https://google.github.io/adk-docs/sessions/) demonstrates explicit session/state/memory decomposition. citeturn13search2\n\n[Temporal — Workflow Execution](https://docs.temporal.io/workflow-execution) and [Temporal — Durable AI](https://docs.temporal.io/ai) establish the current durable-execution baseline against which Continuum's recovery claims should be measured. citeturn13search3turn13search21\n\n[Anthropic — Building Effective Agents](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents) provides a useful mechanism-first taxonomy of workflows and autonomous agent systems. citeturn14search1\n\n[Anthropic — Effective Harnesses for Long-Running Agents](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-harnesses-for-long-running-agents) is directly relevant to Continuum's long-horizon positioning. citeturn14search17\n\n[Anthropic — Multi-Agent Research System](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system) provides an important current multi-agent baseline. citeturn14search9\n\n[Anthropic — Demystifying Evals for AI Agents](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/demystifying-evals-for-ai-agents) reinforces the need to evaluate agentic systems across multi-step, stateful behavior rather than relying on capability descriptions. citeturn14search11\n\n**Security and provenance**\n\n[GitHub Artifact Attestations](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/security-for-github-actions/using-artifact-attestations) provides the native provenance mechanism for release artifacts. citeturn6search0turn6search4\n\n[SLSA](https://slsa.dev/) provides the supply-chain provenance framework useful for defining stronger release guarantees. citeturn6search9turn6search17\n\n[Sigstore Cosign](https://docs.sigstore.dev/cosign/) provides artifact signature and attestation verification tooling when distribution requirements extend beyond GitHub-native provenance. citeturn6search2turn6search6\n\n[OpenSSF Scorecard](https://github.com/ossf/scorecard-action) provides an automated repository security-health signal. citeturn5search1\n\n[Shields.io](https://shields.io/) should be used narrowly for machine-derived custom status rather than decorative badge accumulation. citeturn5search21turn5search3\n\n**Accessibility and scholarly publication**\n\n[W3C WCAG](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/contrast-minimum.html) provides the relevant contrast requirements for custom README assets and interactive documentation. citeturn7search5turn7search1\n\n[GitHub — CITATION files](https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/managing-your-repositorys-settings-and-features/customizing-your-repository/about-citation-files) provides structured citation support. citeturn8search2\n\n[Zenodo GitHub integration](https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/archiving-a-github-repository/referencing-and-citing-content) is appropriate when DCA specifications, datasets, benchmark artifacts, or stable releases require DOI-backed archival citation. citeturn10search34\n\nThe resulting publication strategy is deliberately narrower and stronger than a conventional “visionary” README: **make Continuum visually immediate, technically deep, executable where possible, explicit about uncertainty, and relentlessly evidence-linked.** That presentation creates institutional authority from inspectable engineering rather than authority-signaling language."]},"status":"finished_successfully","end_turn":true,"weight":1.0,"metadata":{"finish_details":{"type":"stop","stop_tokens":[200002]},"is_complete":true,"content_references":[{"matched_text":"citeturn1search2","prefix":null,"start_idx":872,"end_idx":891,"safe_urls":["https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/managing-your-repositorys-settings-and-features/customizing-your-repository/about-readmes","https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/managing-your-repositorys-settings-and-features/customizing-your-repository/about-readmes?utm_source=chatgpt.com"],"refs":[],"alt":"([docs.github.com](https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/managing-your-repositorys-settings-and-features/customizing-your-repository/about-readmes?utm_source=chatgpt.com))","prompt_text":null,"type":"grouped_webpages","items":[{"title":"About the r